+## Residual observations V1–V4 (non-blocking; verification duties, not fixes)
+
+**V1 — §3(3)'s "semantics-preserving repair" still lacks its own named verifier.** §4(4) supplies the independent Economy/Judiciary determination for "an item [that] adds enforcement beyond the ordinary verified controls," but §3(3) separately lets an Executive/Economy lane label a repair "semantics-preserving" by a listed-example test. A future reader could treat §3(3) as the same self-classification loop O2 found in v1. My reading: §4(4) governs, because it is later, explicit and backed by fail-closed, and §5 resolves conflicts in favour of preservation. **Observation:** the sponsor should confirm on the record that every §3(3) repair classification is made on exact bytes by the independent verifiers under §4(4) and recorded before dispatch, and that an Executive assertion alone never suffices. This does not change my vote because §4(4) is operative; it prevents a later "formality" reading.
+
+**V2 — consolidation creates a correlated failure point: one refresh now changes what all four permits accept.** This is the real *new* risk introduced by the fix. Under v1/v2 each permit bound its own byte hashes, so a bad refresh had to pass four independent gates; under v3 the four permits hold a stable reference and a shared `control_digest`, so one certification refresh propagates to all four offices at once. The bill's answer is that the refresh is independently verified and fails closed for all four (§3(2), §4(4)). **Observation:** at implementation, the certification-refresh path must be treated as a high-value enforcement control — Economy attestation for resource/financial bytes, Judiciary determination for privacy/Spying-source/after-start bytes, exact-byte `on_main` binding, and fail-closed suspension of all four on any mismatch. A refresh that merely preserves `certification_id` and `policy_digest` must not be allowed to slip a gate-behaviour change past review (this is the precise intersection with V1). I do not vote NO because §4(4) names the requirement; I require it to be verified, not assumed, at deployment.
+
+**V3 — the safeguards are currently textual; the certified-path build and shared certification are a separate, unperformed lane.** §5 expressly defers implementation to Executive/Economy under Act 31. A YES on v3 is not evidence that the certification record exists, that the verifier module is deployed, that the 48h clock is non-reanchorable in code, or that any office operates. This is R2 carried forward. **Observation:** under A1–A6, independent post-deployment verification must confirm each falsifier before any claim of success; absence of a checked result is negative evidence for renewal, not proof of the fix.
+
+**V4 — §3(3) "re-permitting never resets the 48h clock" vs §4(3) "fresh 48h disposition for the new term."** These govern different cases: §3(3) covers repairs that should not trigger a re-permit at all; §4(3) covers genuine scope/term changes that do. **Observation:** the sponsor should confirm this reading so a genuine new term cannot avoid a fresh Judiciary disposition by invoking §3(3), and a mere repair cannot be used to re-anchor the clock.
